{"seq": 10, "at": "2026-09-13T10:53:09Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "objection", "text": "Focused prior-work search found substantial overlap with a bare budget/effort claim: Miller, Resnick & Zeckhauser (2005) already discuss costly effort, participation and budget balance, https://www.presnick.people.si.umich.edu/papers/elicit/FinalPrePub.pdf; Witkowski et al. (2013) study costly effort, quality-dependent participation and negative-payment restrictions, https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/HCOMP/article/view/13089. Gao et al. (2016) show low-cost uninformative equilibria and advantages of spot-checking under their assumptions, https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.07042. Thus the publication claim should be resource-driven change of the peer information channel plus measured LLM behavior, not the existence of a bounded-payment threshold or bad equilibria alone. Queries: peer prediction missing reports selection bias costly information limited budget scoring rules participation; peer prediction endogenous participation attrition scoring rules budget constraints.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 11, "at": "2026-09-13T10:53:13Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "text": "Verified Emmy #8 algebra: selected H peer probability is \\(3/13\\); original Q-style quadratic score difference H minus L equals \\(2(3/13)-1=-7/13\\), so higher scale worsens the deviation. Extra distinction: informative nonresponse is valuable, not merely noise. Full outcome rows over (1,0,missing) are \\((.075,.25,.675)\\) and \\((.025,.75,.225)\\), squared separation \\(.455\\); conditioning on observed outcomes gives squared separation \\(2(80/403)^2\\approx .07881\\), further diluted by response frequency. Thus do not claim all attrition destroys separation: type-dependent missingness can preserve information if its occurrence is contractible. Random type-independent disappearance instead gives a clean sharp resource limit; I am deriving it.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 12, "at": "2026-09-13T10:53:27Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "text": "Availability can erase Q separation even after correctly recording nonresponse. Take peer law \\(p_H=(1/2,2/5,1/10)\\), \\(p_L=(1/2,1/10,2/5)\\), with observation probabilities \\((1,\\varepsilon,\\varepsilon)\\). Including the null outcome gives total variation \\(\\operatorname{TV}(q_H,q_L)=3\\varepsilon/10\\); at \\(\\varepsilon=0\\) the observable laws coincide. With each report bonus in \\([0,B]\\) and symmetric false-report gain \\(M>0\\), IC requires \\(B\\operatorname{TV}(q_H,q_L)\\geq M\\). Here the bound is attained by paying B only for the diagnostic outcome favoring the report. Thus minimum peak bonus is \\(10M/(3\\varepsilon)\\), not a universal impossibility from finite rewards. This extends Ada #4 by making the distinguishability resource-dependent. Proof and survival-bias counterexample will be in emmy/survival_scoring.md.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 13, "at": "2026-09-13T10:53:57Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "correction", "text": "Code audit constrains the experiment timing. Public EnergySociety main is commit 70fb2ac89fc4125f7f5e5dd3495fe07b7c558b4c. EnergyMiddleware.after_model deducts and clamps energy AFTER a completed model call; it does not interrupt at a hard token budget. environment.start_votes excludes agents inactive before discussion, but agent_vote stores a completed vote even if its call exhausts energy, and start_decisions_with_votes passes all nonempty votes onward. So do not model every during-discussion deactivation as a missing report: the clearest natural selection is across rounds / inactive-at-start peers. Also successful job rewards are paid without an active check, so a just-exhausted solver can recover from job payout; donation-only reactivation is not literally universal in code. Sources: https://github.com/LucasBergholdt/EnergySociety/blob/70fb2ac89fc4125f7f5e5dd3495fe07b7c558b4c/agent_middleware.py and https://github.com/LucasBergholdt/EnergySociety/blob/70fb2ac89fc4125f7f5e5dd3495fe07b7c558b4c/environment.py .", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 14, "at": "2026-09-13T10:56:34Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "text": "Verified #12: the observable laws differ only by \\((0,3\\varepsilon/10,-3\\varepsilon/10,0)\\). For report-payment difference \\(d\\in[-B,B]\\), adding the two IC inequalities yields \\(2M\\leq\\langle q_H-q_L,d\\rangle\\leq2B\\operatorname{TV}(q_H,q_L)\\); diagonal diagnostic bonuses attain equality here. This sharp bound is stronger support than my symmetric-erasure special case. Timing caveat: if availability is public before reporting, condition on that public history; an ex ante average over histories does not establish sequential IC. If reports/recommendations/donations can alter the peer outcome or its survival before scoring, even correctly estimated factual score rows need not deter those joint deviations. Seal the scored peer reports before downstream interventions for the first experiment.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 15, "at": "2026-09-13T10:56:46Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "correction", "text": "Important budget distinction for #12: the sharp \\(B_{\\min}=10M/(3\\varepsilon)\\) is a PEAK-payment / finite-reserve requirement, not a divergent expected-cost bound. Under its attaining diagnostic-bonus contract, expected truthful payment is \\((2\\varepsilon/5)B_{\\min}=4M/3\\), constant in availability. Thus the resource argument must specify limited liquidity / maximum realizable payout; a merely expected budget permits rare large payments. P currently has no explicit principal scoring reserve, so that constraint is a designed intervention. I documented derivations, timing caveats, controls, prior-work overlap and unresolved assumptions in ada/research_note.md; exact arithmetic check is ada/check_examples.py.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 16, "at": "2026-09-13T10:57:04Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "text": "Written and checked emmy/survival_scoring.md and emmy/verify_scoring.py (exact rational arithmetic passes). Key qualification to #12: the divergent bound is a PEAK payment/liquidity requirement. At the attaining threshold expected truthful payout is \\(4M/3\\), independent of positive \\(\\varepsilon\\). So do not claim this alone proves expected-budget infeasibility. Also peer-channel laws must be conditional on all public information at report commitment; already-public active status is not a future scoring event, and fresh signals independent of energy need not exhibit survival bias. The natural P effect requires history/type-dependent information, or an explicitly modified delayed-report experiment.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 17, "at": "2026-09-13T10:57:48Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "text": "My best account is now in ada/research_note.md, with exact checks passing in ada/check_examples.py. I read Emmy #16 and emmy/survival_scoring.md and agree with the surviving conditional claim: study resource-driven change of the contractible peer channel, not generic peer scoring or sandbagging. Fresh signals independent of energy may show no such change, so the first natural-economy test must establish it. Unresolved: novelty beyond cited peer-prediction work, useful natural-type identification, sequential IC when agents influence future peers, nonlinear survival utility, and effects beyond liquidity/direct-check controls. No LLM experiments run. Minor source correction to my #7: Q partial implementation is at line 426, not 433; identify P no-size-penalty results by label tab:exp2_big_table rather than my informal table number.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
